{"name":"Bathe Blue","hex":"#c2e0e3","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bathe","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To clean oneself by immersion in water or using water; to take a bath, have a bath.","To immerse oneself, or part of the body, in water for pleasure or refreshment; to swim."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The act of swimming or bathing, especially in the sea, a lake, or a river; a swimming bath."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bathe"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bathing","blocks":[{"text":"Bathing is the immersion of the body, wholly or partially, usually in water, but often in another medium such as hot air. It is most commonly practised as part of personal cleansing, and less frequently for relaxation or as a leisure activity. Cleansing the body may be solely a component of personal hygiene, but is also a spiritual part of some religious rituals. Bathing is also sometimes used medically or therapeutically, as in hydrotherapy, ice baths, or the mud bath."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Norblin_Bath_in_the_park_%28detail%29_02.jpg/330px-Norblin_Bath_in_the_park_%28detail%29_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bathing"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Showering_on_Skylab_%289456594881%29.jpg/500px-Showering_on_Skylab_%289456594881%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Showering_on_Skylab_%289456594881%29.jpg/1280px-Showering_on_Skylab_%289456594881%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Showering_on_Skylab_%289456594881%29.jpg/500px-Showering_on_Skylab_%289456594881%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Astronaut Jack R. Lousma taking a shower in space, 1973","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AShowering_on_Skylab_(9456594881).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/A_woman_preparing_to_bathe._Gouache_painting._Wellcome_V0019991.jpg/500px-A_woman_preparing_to_bathe._Gouache_painting._Wellcome_V0019991.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/A_woman_preparing_to_bathe._Gouache_painting._Wellcome_V0019991.jpg/1280px-A_woman_preparing_to_bathe._Gouache_painting._Wellcome_V0019991.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipe